Hello, internet! Welcome to Film Theory.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:28.940
Now, you guys all know how much I love movies from video games, right?

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.980
*Laughing*

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:33.660
Sorry,

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.720
I just make myself laugh sometimes.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.320
Anyway,
this one's a little bit different, since

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.560
today, we're talking about Hitman,

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.600
which
is much more "Bourne identity" and much less

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.470
"Super Mario the movie."

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:50.000
In case you haven't seen it yet, the
non-spoiler premise of Hitman: Agent 47

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:54.080
is that a top-secret organization has
been modifying human DNA to splice

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:57.180
together the best genes and create
superhumans.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.760
They train their little genetic creations from birth to become the ultimate killing machines,

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:02.420
and

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.180
that's about all I can talk about without
stepping into spoiler territory,

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.280
so, hey! Avert your eyes and go to the movies, then come back and watch this, because

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:14.000
this episode is really, really good. Or
better yet,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:14.780
click on the Film Theory

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:16.897
playlist, rack me up some retention time,

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:17.940
YOU go see the movie,

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:20.760
come back, and then watch this video.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:21.340
we all win!

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:23.360
But mostly me, because that sweet

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:24.920
sweet retention time.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:26.000
Seriously, you're gonna wanna watch this one.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:30.960
Okay, you back?

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.040
Picking popcorn kernels out
of your teeth?

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.400
Wiping artificial butter substitute off your fingers,

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.020
but still getting your keys all greasy?

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:37.760
Good.

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:38.300
On we go.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.580
So Agent 47 is cool and
all, but for me, the most interesting

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:45.460
character with the least interesting
name is John Smith,

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:49.760
who has this bizarre knack for staying alive despite being SHOT.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:50.940
Repeatedly.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.400
In a typical spy movie, you have your villains! ¯\_(ツ)_/¯

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.660
Sure, but they're just regular guys.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.300
When
you finally shoot them

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.700
(which, granted, does take about six scenes and two expensive car chases)

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.480
they do tend to, you know...

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:04.320
die.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.600
In Hitman though, the problem is that when the hero

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.310
shoot the bad guy, John Smith,

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.360
they have to keep shooting him.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.920
Like, this guy seriously cannot die.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.400
BANG! You're dead!

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:15.020
Oh.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.300
No, just kidding, you're...still here!

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:19.680
Oh! Okay, you're back again...

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.580
Discount bullets are the worst!

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:24.000
There's literally one line in the movie

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:26.300
that actually explains how
he's pulling this off.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.790
"He has subdermal body armor."

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:30.740
"Pretty crazy, huh?"

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:33.360
That's it! That's all we
get in terms of exposition.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.000
"Subdermal armor."

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.960
As in, he has a bulletproof shield
that sits under his skin.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:40.280
Yeah.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:41.440
Okay.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:42.580
Nice try.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:45.660
Why doesn't John Smith just say
resurrection stone?

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.500
That would have been just as plausible, right?

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:49.430
Wrong.

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:55.849
Believe it or not, the technology to
create bulletproof skin actually exists.

00:02:55.849 --> 00:02:58.500
Basically, what we're talking about here
is a bulletproof vest.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.400
But instead of wearing it under your clothes, you wear it under your skin.

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:06.980
Okay, what do we know that's bulletproof?
Obviously, old Zachary Q isn't installing

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.010
steel plates under his skin, So we need a
few other ideas.

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:12.300
Let's start with the most famous bullet
stopper:

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.370
Kevlar.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:16.579
It's weird, isn't it, that whenever
someone's like "Oh yes! The shield was

00:03:16.579 --> 00:03:19.879
fortified with kevlar", everyone's
always like "Ah!" "Yes!"

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.420
"Oh!" "Of course" "Kevlar!" "How droll."

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:24.780
As if any
of us actually know what Kevlar is.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:28.060
I mean, we all pretend like we're super
familiar with it because it shows up in

00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:31.730
every episode of Burn Notice, but until
doing the research for this episode,

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:36.500
I, for one, had no idea that it's actually
just really strong plastic.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.880
Not like this plastic, or that plastic,

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.900
but like the Superman of plastic.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:46.600
In fact, it would be stronger than the old "Man of Steel," since it's five times stronger than steel by weight.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:48.660
More importantly than just
strength, though,

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.100
we need to take a look at its tensile strength.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:55.000
Tensile strength
is a measure of a material's resistance to tearing,

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.480
Or, basically tells you how much
force it takes to break through it.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:03.320
Units of tensile strength are
megapascals. MPas. So the MPa of a

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:07.280
piece of armor tells you the force you
need to put on it to break through it.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:14.329
Kevlar has an unbelievably high tensile
strength of about 3,750 megapascals,

00:04:14.329 --> 00:04:17.500
compared with steel, which is about 650.

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:21.300
Making it a super appealing option for bulletproof vests and helmets,

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.960
but not so
much for putting under skin.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:27.400
Even though Kevlar is light, it's nowhere near that
light.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:32.000
And perhaps more importantly, it's still nowhere near as flexible as human skin.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.400
In the movie, we see John Smith ducking, rolling, jumpin' n' jivin'.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.340
In other words, his movement isn't limited

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:40.040
by whatever he's got under there.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:43.940
And by "there" I mean his under skin armor not his Fruit of the Looms.

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:46.800
In other
words, our armor needs to be extremely flexible.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:50.700
It takes at least 20 layers of Kevlar to
stop a bullet, and these layers aren't

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.440
like your normal fabrics.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.780
They're plastic, so no Downy softness
here.

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:58.960
And while it's a lot lighter than steel,
it's still too heavy.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:01.780
We see ol' John Smith running and
jumping at top speed.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:03.780
It's true he's genetically engineered to be strong,

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.300
but it would be a big burden to be slowed down by armor.

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:08.700
With Kevlar vests
weighing in at eight to ten pounds,

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:12.000
Johnny would have a hard time keeping up
with Agent 47.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.880
But if you thought Kevlar was the cutting-edge, ha!

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.920
Someone hasn't been reading their American chemical society publications lately.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.400
No? That one's just me, huh? Ok, fine. Let me
catch you up on what you've been missing.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.789
There are a few new players in the
miracle substance game lately.

00:05:26.789 --> 00:05:28.880
One of which is nanocellulose.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:32.500
Nanocellulose is like the high-tech material for hippies.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:35.940
Made from all natural
organic nano-sized particles that

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:41.099
actually come from wood, algae, or even
bacteria, and are basically purified all

00:05:41.099 --> 00:05:44.370
the way down to the molecular level, then
pressed together to form a crystal

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:49.289
structure that's eight times stronger
than steel, more flexible than plastic,

00:05:49.289 --> 00:05:52.650
and when thin is even see-through.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:56.190
There's also a lot of really cool
applications for nano cellulose, from

00:05:56.190 --> 00:06:00.990
display screens for phones, bendable
organic batteries, and, you guessed it,

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:02.440
bulletproof armor.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:05.700
The tensile strength
of nano cellulose is reported to be

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:08.909
different things based on the
preparation, but by weight,

00:06:08.909 --> 00:06:14.159
it's comparable to Kevlar. And because
it's not plastic, but instead an organic material,

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:18.320
it's more flexible and could
theoretically be implanted under our skin.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:21.260
One of the less intuitive things
that we can gather from the movie is

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:25.020
that the armor John Smith is using has
to be biologically safe.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:29.610
This may be sci-fi, but at a fundamental
level, John Smith still has human DNA,

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:33.080
which means his body is susceptible to
the same things we are.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:37.380
And the human body generally doesn't have a whole
bunch of unidentifiable junk sitting around it.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:39.960
This "au naturale"
armor would seem to make a good

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:43.620
candidate, except for the fact that it's
a bit too "naturale".

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:47.000
In the sense that it could actually react with your body.

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:49.300
Cellulose is a material found in plants.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:53.280
If you remember from fifth grade biology, it makes up plant cell walls.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:55.660
It's in a ton of our foods, and people
eat it all the time,

00:06:55.660 --> 00:07:00.060
because even though we humans can't digest it it makes us feel full.

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:04.440
The way it does that, though is by reacting with what's already in our bodies:

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:05.460
Water.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:09.870
Cellulose is extremely porous, and it
attracts water molecules that stick to

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:11.190
it on every side.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:15.095
Nano cellulose, exposed to water, blows up
to hundreds of times its volume.

00:07:15.095 --> 00:07:19.260
That's why eating things with cellulose
make you feel full. Because literally its

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:21.030
bloating you with water.

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:24.750
if you were to put nanocellulose under
the skin, the water that's in your body

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:28.800
would be absorbed and stretch you out
like a human balloon.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:31.720
Oh my gosh! does this nano cellulose make me look fat?

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:35.280
Even though the armor itself would be
relatively light, the water that pulled

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:41.220
around it would add pounds, and give your
whole body of bloated, pillowy texture.

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:44.960
And definitely not the svelte Johnny
Smith we've come to know and love.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:46.620
But wait! We're not done.

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:51.380
There is, in fact, a miracle substance
that will allow us our perfect

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:54.920
under armor, or I guess, under skin armor:

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:55.840
Graphene.

00:07:55.849 --> 00:08:01.249
Like its compadre, nanocellulose, it's a
carbon-based sheet, but unlike nano cellulose,

00:08:01.249 --> 00:08:02.400
there's nothing else in it.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:06.200
While other materials are a mix of carbon, oxygen, and a couple other elements,

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:08.899
graphene is unique,
because it's made completely out of

00:08:08.899 --> 00:08:14.959
locked carbon crystals that can exist as
thin as one molecule and can be

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:17.200
stacked in layers to form a crystal lattice.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:20.460
If that concept doesn't sound familiar, it should.

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:24.160
In principle, it's the same
structure that's used to build a diamond,

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:26.580
the hardest substance in the world

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:29.360
If you were to wear under skin graphene armor,

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:33.340
you'd literally be wearing diamond
armor under your skin!

00:08:33.349 --> 00:08:38.479
If you're talking tensile strength,
compare Kevlar's 3000 megapascals to

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:42.060
Graphene's 130,000!

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:46.140
And not only that,
compare the weight of Kevlar or steel to graphene,

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:50.420
where a single layer of
graphene covering an entire football field

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:52.640
would weigh less than a gram.

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:56.440
An entire football field! Less than a gram!

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:00.460
And if you're worried about fitting it
under your skin, don't!

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:07.110
Since graphene is truly only one molecule thick, you
would need to stack three million layers

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:09.660
of this stuff to get one millimeter in
height.

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:11.840
Which is mind blowingly thin,

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:13.280
but begs the question:

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:19.300
"How thick does this stuff need to be to fulfill the 'bullet stopping' part of our bulletproof skin theory?"

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:24.280
Well, it turns out that tests are already
being run on graphene using micro-bullets

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:29.020
literally micro pellets shot at
thin layers of graphene to test it's stopping power.

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:32.340
In experiments at Rice University, with micro bullets being shot

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:34.180
at three kilometers per second,

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:37.580
3x faster than an AK-47 bullet,

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:45.000
a 100-nanometer thick layer of graphene stopped the bullets. 100 nanometers!

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:48.720
The way it's stopping these micro bullets
was by stretching and spreading the

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:53.520
impact force out over a wide radius,
which caused the graphene to bend a lot,

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:58.500
sure, but not to break. Yes, it's only been
tested on a micro scale, testing it

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:03.570
against steel shows that even in very
thin layers it works ten times better

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:10.040
than steel armor, with a tiny fraction of
the weight and practically unlimited flexibility.

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:13.940
Which makes it sound like
the perfect fit to our John Smith

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:16.950
mystery, but then what about the human body?

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:21.620
We talked about the bloaty inconvenience of nano cellulose, but is graphene any better?

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:25.780
Actually, yes. Otherwise, this episode
would have a really unsatisfying conclusion.

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:30.660
Because graphene is made of, in theory, a perfectly uniform carbon structure,

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:33.100
it's almost totally nonreactive.

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:35.020
If any of you remember high school chemistry,

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:37.980
it's never really the carbon atoms that are doing anything exciting.

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:41.040
It's always the oxygens, or the
chlorines that are getting all the action.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.700
They're like the swinging singles
of the atomic world.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:47.700
Carbon is like the married friend that doesn't come to the bar with the gang on Friday nights

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:50.860
anymore, because once it's in a molecule,
it's just happy to hang out.

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:55.100
This means that graphene isn't going to be reacting
with water, and it's not going to dissolve.

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:58.140
It's just gonna stay there until you
need it to help avoid death for the

00:10:58.140 --> 00:10:59.560
twelfth time this weekend.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:01.840
Seriously, John Smith, consider your actions.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:05.160
So all in all, when Johnny Smith is
wearing his graphene armor, it's just

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:09.850
sitting in an ultra-thin, ultra flexible sheet of
diamond-like hardness right under his

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:13.810
skin, made completely of carbon, reacting
with nothing except the bullets he's

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:18.339
taking a few times an hour. So graphene
has a tonna superlatives.

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:21.000
You know what else it's the best at?
Conductivity.

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:26.120
Graphene has been classified as the most conductive
material in the world

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:29.440
meaning that would protect the wearer
from bullets better than any other

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:33.500
substance, it's not the kind of thing you'd
want to be wearing in a thunderstorm.

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:36.720
But still, not a bad trade-off for being
indestructible.

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:41.470
So, in the end, what started off as one of
the most improbable villains in an action movie,

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:46.300
John Smith's "subdermal titanium body armor"
turns out to be an awesome example of

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:49.240
cutting edge and completely plausible
tech.

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:52.389
Wait, did they just say subdermal
TITANIUM armor?

00:11:52.389 --> 00:11:55.200
After all this, and he's not even wearing
the stuff?

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:58.220
Ugh. Well, in the end I suppose it was

00:11:58.220 --> 00:12:03.639
just a theory. A Film Theory! and
cut.

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:04.720
Welcome back to the

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:06.640
SUPER AMAZING END CARD TOURNAMENT!

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:11.680
where I'm curious. If you had the chance to
implant yourself with subdermal super

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:13.780
skin like graphene, would you do it?

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:17.360
Like, it's a cool idea in theory, I get it, but seriously,

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:20.500
what are you doing in your
life that you would need something like that?

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:23.080
It's not like you're getting mugged every time you're

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:24.500
headed to the corner store for a gallon of milk,

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:26.980
or if you are, I'm sorry for that joke, and I would also

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:29.720
recommend trying to move.

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:32.600
Please move from your current
residential area.

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:34.380
Anyway, I was just curious what you would do.

00:12:34.390 --> 00:12:37.930
Yes or No? Click on one to cast your vote
and get sent to the channel page where

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:40.600
you can watch all the other nifty videos
we've been making lately.

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:45.160
Digital movies and Frame by Frame have
had some stellar uploads lately, so I'd

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:46.900
go and check out those series if you
haven't yet.

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:49.660
Now if you'll excuse me, I've got a bow tie to tie.

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:53.420
I don't care how smart you are, those
things are tough.

